---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GradeCalculator from '@/components/tools/everyday/GradeCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="grade-calculator">
  <GradeCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to calculate your grade</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        On the <strong>Weighted</strong> tab, copy the categories and weights from your syllabus
        (homework 20%, quizzes 15%, midterm 25%, final 40%) and enter your score in each. Leave ungraded
        categories blank.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the <strong>Points</strong> tab, for classes graded on total points, enter points earned and
        points possible for each assignment.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the <strong>Final needed</strong> tab, enter your current grade, the weight of the final exam
        and your target grade.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Each tab shows the working and a letter grade on the standard US scale. The weighted tab also
      shows the best grade you can still get.
    </p>

    <h2>The weighted grade formula</h2>
    <p><strong>grade = Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σ(weight)</strong></p>
    <p>
      With homework at 92% (weight 20), quizzes at 85% (weight 15) and a midterm at 78% (weight 25):
    </p>
    <p>
      (92 × 20) + (85 × 15) + (78 × 25) = 1,840 + 1,275 + 1,950 = <strong>5,065</strong><br />
      5,065 ÷ (20 + 15 + 25) = 5,065 ÷ 60 = <strong>84.4%</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculator divides by the graded weight, not by 100. This tells you where you stand on the
      work that has been marked, and does not count the unmarked 40% as a zero. The calculator also
      shows that pessimistic figure, next to the best score you can still get.
    </p>

    <h2>What do I need on the final?</h2>
    <p>The final grade is a weighted average of two things, so solving for the exam score gives:</p>
    <p><strong>needed = (target − current × (1 − w)) ÷ w</strong></p>
    <p>
      where <strong>w</strong> is the final's weight as a decimal. With an 84% going in, a final worth
      30% and a 90% target:
    </p>
    <p>needed = (90 − 84 × 0.70) ÷ 0.30 = (90 − 58.8) ÷ 0.30 = <strong>104%</strong></p>
    <p>
      So an A is out of reach without extra credit: the outcome table shows that a perfect final gives
      88.8%. Aim at a grade you can get, or talk to the instructor early.
    </p>

    <h3>How much the final can move your grade</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Final is worth</th>
            <th scope="col">Best case from an 84%</th>
            <th scope="col">Worst case from an 84%</th>
            <th scope="col">Swing</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10%</td><td>85.6%</td><td>75.6%</td><td>10.0 pts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td>87.2%</td><td>67.2%</td><td>20.0 pts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30%</td><td>88.8%</td><td>58.8%</td><td>30.0 pts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>40%</td><td>90.4%</td><td>50.4%</td><td>40.0 pts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50%</td><td>92.0%</td><td>42.0%</td><td>50.0 pts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The swing always equals the weight of the final. Use this to check any answer.
    </p>

    <h2>Weighted versus points-based grading</h2>
    <p>
      In a weighted class, each category has a fixed share of the final grade, for any number of
      assignments in it. Ten homework tasks worth 20% together are worth 2% each. One exam worth 25% is
      worth twelve times one of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a points-based class, all points are added up. A 100-point exam counts five times a 20-point
      quiz, and there are no category weights. Check the syllabus: the same 88% can be an A− in one
      scheme and a B+ in the other.
    </p>

    <h2>Standard US letter grades</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Letter</th>
            <th scope="col">Percentage</th>
            <th scope="col">GPA points</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A+</td><td>97–100</td><td>4.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A</td><td>93–96.99</td><td>4.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A−</td><td>90–92.99</td><td>3.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B+</td><td>87–89.99</td><td>3.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B</td><td>83–86.99</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B−</td><td>80–82.99</td><td>2.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C+</td><td>77–79.99</td><td>2.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C</td><td>73–76.99</td><td>2.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C−</td><td>70–72.99</td><td>1.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D+</td><td>67–69.99</td><td>1.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D</td><td>63–66.99</td><td>1.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D−</td><td>60–62.99</td><td>0.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>F</td><td>Below 60</td><td>0.0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Scales vary. Some schools use a flat 90/80/70/60 split with no plus or minus grades, some set an
      A at 94, and a few use a 7-point scale where an A starts at 93. Set your target percentage from
      your own syllabus.
    </p>

    <h2>Turning percentages into a GPA</h2>
    <p>
      Letter grades convert to grade points, and grade points weighted by credit hours make a GPA. When
      the semester is finished, the <a href="/gpa-calculator">GPA calculator</a> takes your letters and
      credits and gives the semester and cumulative GPA.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Your scores stay in the browser and are not sent to a server.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
